### What is Cyclic Sort?

Cyclic Sort is an in-place linear-time sorting technique that works when the array values form a known contiguous range, typically 1 to n (or 0 to n - 1), and the values are distinct. The key insight is that each value has exactly one correct home index. For a 1-to-n array, value v belongs at index v - 1.

Instead of comparing elements against each other like a comparison sort, you simply carry each value to its rightful slot.

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Values 1..5 shuffled: [3, 1, 5, 4, 2]
// Value 3 belongs at index 2
// Value 1 belongs at index 0
// Value 5 belongs at index 4
// After cyclic sort: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### How does the algorithm work internally?

Keep a pointer `i` at the current position. Compute the target index `j = nums[i] - 1`. If `nums[i]` is not at its target (`nums[i] != nums[j]`), swap `nums[i]` with `nums[j]`. Crucially, do not advance `i` after a swap because the element just moved into position `i` might itself be out of place. Only when `nums[i]` is already correct do you advance `i`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class CyclicSort {

    public static void sort(int[] nums) {
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nums.length) {
            int correctIndex = nums[i] - 1;
            if (nums[i] != nums[correctIndex]) {
                // Swap to home position
                int temp = nums[i];
                nums[i] = nums[correctIndex];
                nums[correctIndex] = temp;
            } else {
                i++; // already in place, move on
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] lockerKeys = {3, 1, 5, 4, 2};
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(lockerKeys));
        sort(lockerKeys);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(lockerKeys));
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [3, 1, 5, 4, 2]
After:  [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### Why is it O(n) despite the nested feel?

Although the while loop and the inner swapping look quadratic, the amortized analysis shows O(n). Each swap places at least one number into its final correct index permanently. Since there are only n numbers, at most n swaps occur across the entire run. The pointer `i` advances n times in total. So the total work is at most 2n steps, which is O(n).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SwapCountDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {5, 4, 3, 2, 1}; // worst case: completely reversed
        int swaps = 0;
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nums.length) {
            int j = nums[i] - 1;
            if (nums[i] != nums[j]) {
                int t = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[j]; nums[j] = t;
                swaps++;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Swaps: " + swaps + " for n=" + nums.length);
    }
}
```

```text
Swaps: 2 for n=5
```

### Why use value comparison instead of index comparison?

Comparing `nums[i] != nums[j]` rather than `i != j` makes the algorithm robust if duplicates ever appear. With duplicates, two positions might both want to hold the same value, and an index comparison would create an infinite swap loop between them. The value comparison detects that the target already holds the correct value and stops.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class DuplicateSafety {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Array with a duplicate (3 appears twice, 2 is missing)
        int[] nums = {3, 1, 3, 5, 4};
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nums.length) {
            int j = nums[i] - 1;
            if (nums[i] != nums[j]) {
                int t = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[j]; nums[j] = t;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));
        // [1, 3, 3, 4, 5] -> index 1 has 3 instead of 2, revealing the duplicate and missing
    }
}
```

```text
[1, 3, 3, 4, 5]
```

### How does this pattern find missing numbers?

After cyclic sorting, every value that could be placed at its correct index is there. Any position where `nums[i] != i + 1` reveals either a missing number (i + 1 is missing) or a duplicate (nums[i] is the duplicate).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MissingNumberFinder {
    public static List<Integer> findMissing(int[] nums) {
        // Cyclic sort
        int i = 0;
        while (i < nums.length) {
            int j = nums[i] - 1;
            if (nums[i] != nums[j]) {
                int t = nums[i]; nums[i] = nums[j]; nums[j] = t;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        // Find mismatches
        List<Integer> missing = new ArrayList<>();
        for (i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] != i + 1) {
                missing.add(i + 1);
            }
        }
        return missing;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {4, 3, 2, 7, 8, 2, 3, 1};
        System.out.println("Missing: " + findMissing(nums));
    }
}
```

```text
Missing: [5, 6]
```

### What are the edge cases?

- **Already sorted**: `[1,2,3,4,5]` makes zero swaps; every element is already home.
- **Single element**: `[1]` is trivially sorted.
- **Two elements swapped**: `[2,1]` needs exactly one swap.
- **Range 0 to n-1**: adjust the home index to `nums[i]` instead of `nums[i] - 1`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class CyclicEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sorted = {1, 2, 3};
        CyclicSort.sort(sorted);
        System.out.println("Already sorted: " + Arrays.toString(sorted));

        int[] single = {1};
        CyclicSort.sort(single);
        System.out.println("Single: " + Arrays.toString(single));

        int[] swapped = {2, 1};
        CyclicSort.sort(swapped);
        System.out.println("Swapped: " + Arrays.toString(swapped));
    }
}
```

```text
Already sorted: [1, 2, 3]
Single: [1]
Swapped: [1, 2]
```

### When should you use Cyclic Sort?

Use Cyclic Sort when the array values form a known dense range (1 to n, 0 to n-1) and you need O(n) time with O(1) space. Do not use it as a general-purpose sort; it only works when each value maps to a unique index.

- Finding the missing number in a 1-to-n array.
- Finding all duplicates in a 1-to-n array.
- Finding the first missing positive integer.
- Sorting sequence numbers that are a known permutation.

### What about the banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, the nightly settlement batch delivers settlement sequence numbers guaranteed to be a permutation of 1 to n but arriving in random order. Rajesh uses Cyclic Sort to arrange them in a single disciplined pass with no extra memory, keeping the reconciliation window fast even on very large batches.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SettlementReconciler {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sequenceNums = {5, 3, 1, 4, 2};
        System.out.println("Received: " + Arrays.toString(sequenceNums));
        CyclicSort.sort(sequenceNums);
        System.out.println("Sorted:   " + Arrays.toString(sequenceNums));
    }
}
```

```text
Received: [5, 3, 1, 4, 2]
Sorted:   [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What if the range is 0 to n-1 instead of 1 to n? (Adjust the home index to `nums[i]` instead of `nums[i] - 1`.)
- What if there are duplicates? (The value comparison guard prevents infinite loops; mismatches after sorting reveal duplicates.)
- Can this sort arbitrary arrays? (No, only arrays with a known dense value range.)
- What is the maximum number of swaps? (n - 1 in the worst case for a derangement.)
